nded, because re-filing them would rewrite what he meant then from what he means now. **P-PASS:** guard `bench/tests/test_frx_is_its_own_kind_2026-11-13.py`, 6 tests including his byte-exact sentence and a discrimination half proving `fr` still means what it meant; mutations `scripts/the_frx_kind_mutations_2026-11-13.py`, **5 of 5 red**, restore verified byte-identical by SHA-256. Documented in both instruction files as he required. ★★★ **VERIFIED 2026-11-14 AND IT FIXED 1 OF THE 2 READERS. THE OTHER ONE LOST HIS FIX REQUEST.** Every claim above re-checked by execution: `frx` is in `KINDS`; the harvester's `MARKER` returns `frx` on his byte-exact sentence; the guard is 6 of 6 green; the harness is 5 of 5 red. **BUT THE VOCABULARY HAS A SECOND READER AND IT WAS NEVER TOUCHED.** `scripts/harvest_tickets_2026-10-17.py:79` IMPORTS `scripts/read_founder_responses_2026-10-11.py` as its reader for `--sources responses`, and that file carries 3 patterns of its own. Driven on his words *"As I said above you should fix both. frx (as you will note this is my first use of the fix request ticket classification.)"*: the harvester returned **`frx`**, the reader's `_MARKER_LINE` returned **None**, its `_MARKER_TRAILING` returned **None**, and `annotations()` reported the marker as **blank** — because the marker is followed by a parenthetical and the trailing pattern required end-of-line. ★★★ **AND THE CONSEQUENCE WAS NOT COSMETIC: HIS FIX REQUEST WAS FILED AS A DISCUSSION AND THE CORRECT TICKET WAS RETIRED.** The harvester's own fallback at line 432 re-applies its `MARKER` when the reader's field is empty, so an `frx` ticket WAS created — `frx:3701741d604b`. But the unmarked reading also produced `disc:4f22c0c038b6` on the same anchor, and when `scripts/a_reclassification_is_not_a_second_ticket_2026-10-30.py` recomputed the kind from the READER it got `disc`, concluded the `frx` row was the stale one, and **DEPRECATED HIS FIX REQUEST**. Read out of the store: `disc:4f22c0c038b6` status UNCONFIRMED, `frx:3701741d604b` status DEPRECATED. **That deprecation was caused by a harvest I ran earlier the same night, and nothing surfaced it** — the Stop gate would have reported his fix request to him as an outstanding DISCUSSION. **REPAIRED AND VERIFIED: exactly 1 live row on that anchor and it is the `frx`.** **THE 3 DEFECTS IN THE SECOND READER, AND THE FIX FOR 2 OF THEM WAS A DELETION RATHER THAN A CHECKER.** (1) The trailing suffix could not see a marker followed by a parenthetical. **Widened, and measured before it was made because adding to an accepting pattern is a LOOSENING: over all 895 of his annotations it changes exactly 1 verdict, 0.001117, Wilson [0.000197, 0.006302], Clopper-Pearson [0.000028, 0.006209] with scipy.beta agreeing to 8.674e-19 — and that 1 is his own sentence. 0 verdicts LOST, 0 false positives in 895.** The reader's tally moved from 721 unmarked and 0 `frx` to 720 and 1, with every other kind unchanged. (2) 2 of its patterns each SPELLED THE 9 KINDS OUT TWICE. They now derive from a single `_KIND_ALT`, which **removes 4 re-spellings** instead of adding a comparator for them — a derived pattern cannot diverge, and `simplicity-default` prefers a deletion to a checker that has to be kept honest. Longest-first ordering makes the `fr`-is-a-prefix-of-`frx` case correct by CONSTRUCTION rather than correct by the engine backtracking. (3) **ITS FALLBACK VOCABULARY HAD GONE STALE, SILENTLY, AND OMITTED `frx`.** `_HIS_KINDS` imports `ticket_store.KINDS` with a literal tuple used only when that import fails — so on exactly the run where nothing else is available to notice, his newest marker became unreadable. Driven both ways on his sentence: READ with the import, INVISIBLE with the fallback. A fallback that is stale is worse than no fallback, because it looks like coverage. **AND THE HARVESTER'S OWN COMMENT UNDERCOUNTED THE DECLARATION SITES BY 3.** It says *4 PLACES SPELL THIS VOCABULARY OUT* and names them; there were **7** — those