-**B184. `frx` IS NOW A TICKET KIND OF ITS OWN, AND HIS FIRST USE OF IT WAS INVISIBLE TO THE MARKER THAT WAS SUPPOSED TO READ IT** Added 2026-11-13T21:40:00+00:00. **STATUS: BUILT, GUARDED AND DOCUMENTED 2026-11-13. HIS RULING. NO FURTHER RULING IS REQUESTED.** **FIND.** His ruling, verbatim: *"There is a potential collision here, and I think it can be cleared up by adding another label to issues in the ticket store. 'fr' to my eyes can sometimes be read as 'feature request', or 'fix request'. What I propose going forward is that a fix request should be marked by me as a 'frx' and a feature request should remain as 'fr'. We only need to do this going forward."* **FOLLOW, AND THE OBVIOUS WORRY WAS THE WRONG ONE.** `fr` is a prefix of `frx`, so a left-to-right alternation looks as though it must swallow the longer token. Driven both ways it does not: Python backtracks when the suffix fails, and `fr|frx` and `frx|fr` return identical answers on every probe. That worry is recorded as REFUTED rather than fixed. **ANALYSE, AND THE REAL DEFECT IS WORSE THAN A COLLISION.** His FIRST USE of the kind was INVISIBLE. `MARKER`'s suffix admitted a colon, a closing bracket, a spaced dash or end-of-line; he wrote `frx (`, so the pattern returned None and the ruling that CREATED the kind would itself have been harvested as unmarked. **A ruling written in the notation it creates must be readable in that notation, or the notation does not exist in practice.** **FIX, AND THE WIDENING WAS MEASURED BEFORE IT WAS MADE**, because adding to an accepting pattern is a LOOSENING and this project has already recorded one that began admitting what nobody intended. Over all **895** of his annotations the widened suffix changes exactly **1** verdict, **0.001117**, Wilson [0.000197, 0.006302], Clopper-Pearson [0.000028, 0.006209] on 2 agreeing routes -- and that 1 is his own `frx` sentence. **0 false positives in 895.** **FORWARD-ONLY, AS HE RULED:** the 311 historical rows under `fr` are NOT re-kinded, because re-filing them would rewrite what he meant then from what he means now. **P-PASS:** guard `bench/tests/test_frx_is_its_own_kind_2026-11-13.py`, 6 tests including his byte-exact sentence and a discrimination half proving `fr` still means what it meant; mutations `scripts/the_frx_kind_mutations_2026-11-13.py`, **5 of 5 red**, restore verified byte-identical by SHA-256. Documented in both instruction files as he required. ★★★ **VERIFIED 2026-11-14 AND IT FIXED 1 OF THE 2 READERS. THE OTHER ONE LOST HIS FIX REQUEST.** Every claim above re-checked by execution: `frx` is in `KINDS`; the harvester's `MARKER` returns `frx` on his byte-exact sentence; the guard is 6 of 6 green; the harness is 5 of 5 red. **BUT THE VOCABULARY HAS A SECOND READER AND IT WAS NEVER TOUCHED.** `scripts/harvest_tickets_2026-10-17.py:79` IMPORTS `scripts/read_founder_responses_2026-10-11.py` as its reader for `--sources responses`, and that file carries 3 patterns of its own. Driven on his words *"As I said above you should fix both. frx (as you will note this is my first use of the fix request ticket classification.)"*: the harvester returned **`frx`**, the reader's `_MARKER_LINE` returned **None**, its `_MARKER_TRAILING` returned **None**, and `annotations()` reported the marker as **blank** — because the marker is followed by a parenthetical and the trailing pattern required end-of-line. ★★★ **AND THE CONSEQUENCE WAS NOT COSMETIC: HIS FIX REQUEST WAS FILED AS A DISCUSSION AND THE CORRECT TICKET WAS RETIRED.** The harvester's own fallback at line 432 re-applies its `MARKER` when the reader's field is empty, so an `frx` ticket WAS created — `frx:3701741d604b`. But the unmarked reading also produced `disc:4f22c0c038b6` on the same anchor, and when `scripts/a_reclassification_is_not_a_second_ticket_2026-10-30.py` recomputed the kind from the READER it got `disc`, concluded the `frx` row was the stale one, and **DEPRECATED HIS FIX REQUEST**. Read out of the store: `disc:4f22c0c038b6` status UNCONFIRMED, `frx:3701741d604b` status DEPRECATED. **That deprecation was caused by a harvest I ran earlier the same night, and nothing surfaced it** — the Stop gate would have reported his fix request to him as an outstanding DISCUSSION. **REPAIRED AND VERIFIED: exactly 1 live row on that anchor and it is the `frx`.** **THE 3 DEFECTS IN THE SECOND READER, AND THE FIX FOR 2 OF THEM WAS A DELETION RATHER THAN A CHECKER.** (1) The trailing suffix could not see a marker followed by a parenthetical. **Widened, and measured before it was made because adding to an accepting pattern is a LOOSENING: over all 895 of his annotations it changes exactly 1 verdict, 0.001117, Wilson [0.000197, 0.006302], Clopper-Pearson [0.000028, 0.006209] with scipy.beta agreeing to 8.674e-19 — and that 1 is his own sentence. 0 verdicts LOST, 0 false positives in 895.** The reader's tally moved from 721 unmarked and 0 `frx` to 720 and 1, with every other kind unchanged. (2) 2 of its patterns each SPELLED THE 9 KINDS OUT TWICE. They now derive from a single `_KIND_ALT`, which **removes 4 re-spellings** instead of adding a comparator for them — a derived pattern cannot diverge, and `simplicity-default` prefers a deletion to a checker that has to be kept honest. Longest-first ordering makes the `fr`-is-a-prefix-of-`frx` case correct by CONSTRUCTION rather than correct by the engine backtracking. (3) **ITS FALLBACK VOCABULARY HAD GONE STALE, SILENTLY, AND OMITTED `frx`.** `_HIS_KINDS` imports `ticket_store.KINDS` with a literal tuple used only when that import fails — so on exactly the run where nothing else is available to notice, his newest marker became unreadable. Driven both ways on his sentence: READ with the import, INVISIBLE with the fallback. A fallback that is stale is worse than no fallback, because it looks like coverage. **AND THE HARVESTER'S OWN COMMENT UNDERCOUNTED THE DECLARATION SITES BY 3.** It says *4 PLACES SPELL THIS VOCABULARY OUT* and names them; there were **7** — those 4 plus 3 in the reader it imports, which no comment there mentioned. **The cost is measured, not feared: the commit board of 2026-11-13 refused with 6 failures until the unnamed sites were found by hand.** Corrected, and after the derivation the live figure is **5**: the harvester's 3, `KINDS`, and the reader's fallback tuple. **P-PASS: `bench/tests/test_the_reader_vocabulary_cannot_drift_2026-11-14.py`, 11 tests, on `hooks/pre-commit`; `scripts/the_reader_vocabulary_mutations_2026-11-14.py`, 7 of 7 red, subject restored SHA-256 identical.** The import-failure path is EXECUTED by making `bench.ticket_store` unimportable, so the fallback literal cannot rot again. **2 OF THE 7 MUTATIONS WERE WRONG BEFORE THE GUARD WAS**, and both are recorded rather than quietly fixed. One reverted the alternation to shortest-first expecting `fr` to shadow `frx`; the guard stayed green and was RIGHT, because Python backtracks — which B184 had already measured and recorded as REFUTED — so there was nothing to catch and it was replaced. The other dropped the leading word boundary expecting `frxy` to read as a marker; it does not, because the trailing suffix already rejects it. **What that boundary actually protects is a word ENDING in a kind: without it `"this is unfr"` reads as `fr` and `"we codisc"` reads as `disc`.** The guard tested only the starting case, so the mutation exposed a missing assertion rather than a broken fix — the 3rd time in this window a harness found an absent test instead of a defect.
